==English==
{{was wotd|2018|April|18}}

===Etymology===
Origin uncertain; conjectured to be from {{der|en|nl|[[dit]] [[voor]] [[dat]]||this for that}}, {{der|en|fr|[[tant]] [[pour]] [[tant]]||equal for equal}},<ref>{{cite-book|entry=tit|editors=E[lizabeth] M[cLaren] Kirkpatrick and C[atherine] M. Schwarz|title=The Wordsworth Dictionary of Idioms|series=Wordsworth Reference Series|location=Ware, Hertfordshire|publisher={{w|Wordsworth Reference}}|year=1993|page=391|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8C7k7ZW3dIEC&pg=PA391|isbn=978-1-85326-309-5}}.</ref> or {{cog|en|tip for tap||a blow for a blow}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|GA}} {{IPA|/ˈtɪt fə(ɹ) ˈtæt/|lang=en}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} [[equivalent|Equivalent]] [[retribution]], [[return#Verb|returning]] [[exactly]] what one gets; [[an eye for an eye]].
#: {{ux|en|If you hit me, I’ll hit you back; '''tit for tat'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=[Edward Ward]|authorlink=Ned Ward|title=The Dutch Riddle: Or, A Character of a Hairy Monster, often Found in Holland, &c.|location=[London?]|publisher=Sold by the booksellers of London and Westminster|year=c. 1706|page=2|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hbZaAAAAcAAJ&pg=PP8|oclc=66791273|passage=Could it but for a longer Space / Lengthen the Bliſs it let's us taſte, / Who would not [[doat]] on't? But alas! / The Joy's too exquiſite to laſt. / Two white ''Herculean'' Pillars prop / The tufted Gin, the tempting Snare; / When they divide, then in we pop, / Before we well know where we are. / Then that for this, and '''tit for tat'''; / But when the pleaſing Minute's flown, / As uſeleſs it returns the Bait, / And both look fooliſh when 'tis done.}}

